  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "ZLjuJUuAsp66kR7ruh9bmOdoB44=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:27:09.955Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "The mechanism is a Tryton model hook dispatching typed operations on workflow transition. The distinctive part of this group is not the hook but the sizing judgement attached to it: the work is small enough for a one-person shop to ship as a module.\n\nThat reframes the deliverable. For larger partners the output of an integration is a reference implementation the vendor maintains; here it is a Tryton module the partner can own, publish and install for their own customers. The scope has to stay inside what a single developer can carry, which constrains the design as much as any technical requirement does.\n\nThree leads carry this demo \u2014 Wuerfel Datentechnik, m-ds and virtual things, all German \u2014 one at \"2 \u00b7 next\" and two at \"3 \u00b7 later\". Small partners are usually screened out on co-build capacity, so an integration deliberately scoped to their size is the mechanism by which this part of the corpus becomes reachable at all.\n\n---\n\n## Leads sharing this finding (3)\n\n**2 \u00b7 next**\n- Wuerfel Datentechnik (DE Sinsheim) \u2014 Lars W\u00fcrfel, Inhaber \u00b7 info@wuerfel-datentechnik.de \u00b7 https://wuerfel-datentechnik.de\n\n**3 \u00b7 later**\n- m-ds (DE Teltow) \u2014 https://m-ds.de\n- virtual things (DE Munich/Aachen) \u2014 https://virtual-things.biz\n\nVerbatim research text this claim was extracted from:\n\n> Tryton model hook dispatching typed operations on workflow transition \u2014 small enough for a one-person shop to ship as a module.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-19T11:27:09.955392Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:27:09.955396Z"
    }
  },
